  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 245 PM CDT THU OCT 22 2026\n\nThis afternoon at the surface, a large area of high pressure is\npresent across much of the eastern half of Conus. In the upper\nlevels, northerly flow is present. Dry conditions will persist\nthrough the seven day forecast with warm days and cool nights. A\nsurface low will develop over the Northern US Rockies now into\nFriday and move northeast into Central Canada by Saturday. As it\ndoes so, the associated front will extend out to the south from\nit and push eastward out of the Rockies and into the Plains.\nAlso, upper ridging will start out over on the lee side of the\nRockies and traverse through the Plains into Eastern Conus by the\nend of the weekend. All that said, we will remain in a dry\npattern with little available moisture for cloud cover much less\nrain. We may have some meager moisture for a little cloud cover\nby the beginning of next week. By Monday, an upper shortwave\nmoving northeast out of the Western US will help to weaken the\nEastern Conus ridge yielding south to southwest upper flow and a\nreturn to some higher cloud cover. Temperatures will remain\nfairly similar over the next week with lows in the 40s and highs\ngenerally in the 70s across Central Alabama.\n\n08",
